{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>Rejoice, O nations, with Him.</b> The language of lamentation, like ״Arise, cry out in the night״ — wail over the nations that subjugated Israel. Why? <b>For the blood of His servants He will avenge.</b> Another interpretation: <b>rejoice</b> in the language of praise — <b>rejoice, O nations,</b> before HaShem, and who are the nations? Israel, <b>with Him</b> — for indeed <b>the blood of His servants He will avenge.</b> Another interpretation: all the nations shall rejoice and praise with Him, so to speak, of HaShem — for indeed He exacts retribution from their oppressors, that is, <b>for the blood of His servants He will avenge.</b>" "<b>And He will atone.</b> In the language of appeasement, like ״that I may appease His face.״" "<b>His land, His people.</b> There are words that are defective and have a vav at their beginning, like וחללה, זונה, שמש, ירח, עמד, זבולה. So too here, אדמתו עמו is like אדמתו ועמו — that is, He will favor and appease His land and His people by avenging their enemies."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The reference is to words in biblical poetry where a prefixed vav functions as a paragogic or recessive element; the examples cited are from biblical poetic passages (e.g., Judges 5) where forms like וחללה are traditionally understood as though the vav were a conjunction prefixed to the following construct chain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:21.129626075Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}